{"version":1,"meta":{"title":"How a Rainbow Forms","subject":"Physics","language":"en","mood":"curious"},"canvas":{"logicalWidth":1000,"theme":"light"},"beats":[{"id":"b1","narration":"Sunlight looks white, but a raindrop can split it into every colour you can see.","ops":[{"id":"h1","type":"heading","text":"How a rainbow forms","level":1},{"id":"fig1","type":"figure","frame":{"width":800,"height":360},"parts":[{"name":"sun","cue":0,"marks":[{"kind":"circle","cx":110,"cy":180,"r":50,"color":"orange","fill":"#ffe08a"},{"kind":"label","x":110,"y":260,"text":"Sun","size":20,"align":"middle"}]},{"name":"earth","cue":1,"marks":[{"kind":"circle","cx":640,"cy":180,"r":60,"color":"blue","fill":"#bcd9f7"},{"kind":"label","x":640,"y":270,"text":"Earth","size":20,"align":"middle"}]},{"name":"light","cue":2,"marks":[{"kind":"arrow","x1":160,"y1":180,"x2":580,"y2":180,"color":"orange"},{"kind":"label","x":370,"y":160,"text":"sunlight","size":18,"align":"middle"}]},{"name":"pull","cue":3,"marks":[{"kind":"arrow","x1":640,"y1":100,"x2":640,"y2":40,"color":"red","dashed":true},{"kind":"label","x":660,"y":60,"text":"heat rises","size":16,"align":"start"}]},{"name":"diag","cue":4,"marks":[{"kind":"arrow","x1":160,"y1":230,"x2":590,"y2":230,"color":"green"},{"kind":"arrow","x1":700,"y1":300,"x2":690,"y2":236,"color":"purple"}]}]}]},{"id":"b2","narration":"Light enters the drop and slows down, which bends it. Red bends the least, violet bends the most.","ops":[{"id":"fig2","type":"figure","frame":{"width":820,"height":340},"parts":[{"name":"incoming","cue":1,"marks":[{"kind":"path","d":"M20 140 L240 150","color":"ink","width":3},{"kind":"label","x":20,"y":110,"text":"white light","size":20,"align":"start"}]},{"name":"drop","cue":3,"marks":[{"kind":"circle","cx":330,"cy":170,"r":100,"color":"blue","fill":"#e8eefe"}]},{"name":"red_ray","cue":10,"marks":[{"kind":"path","d":"M400 210 L790 260","color":"red","width":4},{"kind":"label","x":640,"y":215,"text":"Red bends least","size":20,"align":"middle"}]},{"name":"violet_ray","cue":14,"marks":[{"kind":"path","d":"M400 215 L790 320","color":"purple","width":4},{"kind":"label","x":560,"y":335,"text":"Violet bends most","size":20,"align":"middle"}]}]}]},{"id":"b3","narration":"This bending by colour is called dispersion. Different colours leave at slightly different angles.","ops":[{"id":"d1","type":"definition","term":"Dispersion","definition":"Light of different colours bending by different amounts as it passes through a material."},{"id":"c1","type":"callout","tone":"key","text":"One white beam in, a whole spectrum out."}]},{"id":"b4","narration":"Red leaves at about forty two degrees and violet at about forty, so red ends up on the outside of the bow.","ops":[{"id":"t1","type":"table","columns":["Colour","Angle from the sun's opposite direction"],"rows":[["Red","about 42 degrees"],["Green","about 41 degrees"],["Violet","about 40 degrees"]]}]}],"checks":[{"question":"Which colour bends the least inside a raindrop?","options":["Violet","Red","Green"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Red slows down and bends the least, so it leaves at the largest angle and sits on the outside of the rainbow."},{"question":"What is the splitting of white light into colours called?","options":["Dispersion","Reflection","Absorption"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dispersion is the colour-dependent bending of light."}]}